---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Philosophy — John Morgan"
  description="John Morgan's engineering philosophy: problem solving, code review, agentic development, and how he leads teams."
>
  <SiteHeader active="philosophy" />

  <main>
    <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; back home</a>
    <span class="eyebrow">Philosophy</span>
    <h1 class="page-intro">How I think about engineering</h1>
    <p>
      The short version is on the <a href="/">homepage</a>. This is the long version — the actual
      operating principles, written down the way I've shared them with teams I've led.
    </p>

    <section class="detail-block">
      <h3>Solve it yourself first, then escalate well</h3>
      <p>
        My default assumption when I hit a problem: I'm on my own until I've exhausted every
        option available to me. That doesn't mean working in isolation — it means taking full
        ownership of finding a solution before asking someone else to spend their time on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I learned this the hard way, working early in my career with a brilliant but very
        time-constrained senior engineer. Getting a response from them took days unless the
        question was sharp and clearly worth their limited time. So I got disciplined: understand
        the problem deeply and write it down, enumerate every possible action before taking one,
        weigh each by effort versus likelihood of success, work the list systematically, and only
        escalate once I'd genuinely run out of road — arriving with what I'd tried, what I'd
        learned, and a concrete ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        This isn't about being a hero or suffering in silence. Production outages, hard blocks on
        someone else's work, and genuine learning opportunities are all good reasons to break from
        the process and ask immediately. The point is resourcefulness, not martyrdom — and when
        you do ask, you make the conversation as short and effective as possible for the person
        helping you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-block">
      <h3>Code review should make the team faster, not slower</h3>
      <p>
        Code review exists to improve long-term velocity, not to catch every bug or produce
        perfect code. If it's making a team slower in the short term without paying that back
        later, something about the process is broken. What actually pays off: socializing the
        codebase so more people can work in any part of it, catching drift from agreed norms
        before it compounds, and turning solo work into a shared understanding of the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means small PRs over big ones — review quality visibly drops once a
        change is too large to hold in your head — and reviewing others' work before starting your
        own, because a PR sitting in a queue is worse for the team than almost anything else. It
        means using "nit:" and "out of scope:" prefixes so authors know what's actually blocking,
        asking curious questions instead of making demands, and getting on a call after two rounds
        of comment ping-pong instead of letting it drag on in a thread. And it means fixing
        recurring nitpicks with a linter or a style guide entry instead of repeating them by hand
        forever.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-block">
      <h3>Building agentically</h3>
      <p>
        Coding agents crossed a real threshold — not just generating code, but taking a task from
        plan to shipped change with minimal hand-holding. Teams that adapt their workflow around
        that capability move meaningfully faster than teams that just use the tools as a faster
        autocomplete. I've led that shift directly: introducing engineers to agentic tools,
        pushing internal adoption through talks and pairing, and helping non-engineering teams
        pick up the same tools for their own work.
      </p>
      <p>
        The unglamorous prerequisites matter more than the tools themselves. Agents need fast,
        reliable tests so they can confirm their own changes work — without that, you have
        AI-assisted programming, not agentic engineering. They need clear, tiered permissions for
        what they can touch (local edits are fine to hand off freely; production access is not).
        And they need living documentation that the team actually updates when something goes
        wrong, the same discipline you'd expect around any other institutional knowledge — an
        agent that repeats a mistake because nobody wrote down the fix is a process failure, not a
        model failure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standardization compounds here more than usual. Agents — and the humans reviewing their
        output — work best when every ticket, PR, and pattern in a codebase could have been
        written by anyone. Two slightly different "good" ways of doing the same thing cost more
        than committing to one, even when the other option was arguably a little better.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-block">
      <h3>How I lead</h3>
      <p>
        I try to be a hands-off manager by default: I'm here to help people think through hard
        problems, clarify priorities, and give feedback — not to hand out step-by-step
        instructions for work someone is equipped to figure out themselves. Ownership matters more
        to me than process compliance. A ticket isn't done when it matches the spec; it's done
        when the outcome the spec was trying to produce actually happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        I lean hard on writing things down and async communication — a doc that anyone can read
        later beats a conversation that only the people in the room remember, and it usually
        produces a better answer besides, since writing forces you to actually think the idea
        through. If a disagreement will take more than a couple of back-and-forths in writing,
        that's the signal to get on a call — but come prepared, so the call is short.
      </p>
      <p>
        And once a decision is made, the team commits to it together rather than each person
        quietly running their own variant. A team that's 100% aligned on a good decision moves
        faster than one that's split between two slightly-better ones.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
